Raw threads, and runnable.

The most direct way to run code on another thread is the thread class itself. You'll rarely use it directly in modern code — pools and executors, in the next section, are almost always better — but understanding the raw mechanism is essential, because everything above it is built on this. A thread needs two things: some code to run, and the act of starting it.

The code to run is expressed as a runnable. Runnable is a functional interface, from module six, with one method, run, taking nothing and returning nothing. Because it's a single-abstract-method interface, it's just a lambda. So runnable task equals open-paren close-paren, arrow, print "running on", and the current thread. Runnable is the "unit of work" abstraction. On its own, it's inert — a runnable is just an object holding some behaviour. Something has to actually run it.

And here's how you start it — and this is the single most important detail in the section. You wrap the runnable in a thread, and you call start. So thread t equals new thread of task, then t-dot-start. Start asks the java virtual machine and the operating system to create a new thread, and invoke run on it. Your current thread keeps going, and the two now run concurrently. Now, watch out: calling run directly, t-dot-run, is a classic beginner trap. It just invokes the method on the current thread, like any ordinary call, with zero concurrency. So remember: start spawns; run doesn't.

Once started, threads are scheduled by the operating system, and you get no ordering guarantee between them. So if you start one thread that prints A, and another that prints B, and then main prints "main" — the possible outputs include "main A B", "A B main", "B main A", and more. The order changes from run to run. This nondeterminism is the essential character of concurrency: you cannot assume one thread reaches a point before another, unless you make it so.

To make the current thread wait for another to finish, you call join. It blocks until that thread's run returns. So thread t, start it, then t-dot-join — main pauses right there until t is done — and then you print "t has finished". Join is the most basic form of coordination: don't proceed until that work is complete. And note that join throws interrupted-exception — the checked signal that a blocked thread is being asked to stop. That's a first hint that blocking calls, and interruption, are part of the model.

So why will you rarely write this by hand? Raw thread works, but it's the assembly language of concurrency, and it has real drawbacks. First, it's unbounded and expensive — new-thread per task means no limit, and about a megabyte each; spawn one per request, and a busy server runs out of memory. Second, there's no result, no pooling, and no lifecycle — runnable returns nothing, threads aren't reused, and you hand-manage every start and join.

Every one of these is solved by the executor-service — a managed pool that you submit tasks to, which reuses threads, and hands back a future for the result. That's the next section, and it's how real code runs concurrent work. Raw thread is the foundation to understand; the executor is the tool you'll actually reach for.
